    "passage": "We are no strangers to the Doppler effect, even if we don’t know it by name: an ambulance [[1]] past, and its blaring siren drops in pitch. The Doppler effect is a change in the wavelength of light or sound waves, which is caused by the source of the waves moving toward or away from an observer. If a wave source moves toward an observer, then this observer [[2]] shorter waves than the source actually emits. If a wave source moves away from an observer, the [[3]] is true. In the case of sound, the faster the object, the greater the pitch change.\nOne of the fields where the Doppler Effect plays a(n) [[4]] role is astronomy. Stars and other celestial objects [[5]] light waves. When a celestial object moves toward Earth, its light waves appear bunched up—crowded in smaller circles. These shorter light waves look bluer—a(n) [[6]] known as blueshift. When an object moves away from Earth, its light waves seem stretched out and look redder, [[7]] the term redshift. Blueshift and redshift can [[8]] star motions, helping astronomers detect the gravitational pull of planets.\nThe Doppler effect also helps the police catch drivers who are speeding. Radar devices are pointed at cars, and radio waves would then [[9]] moving vehicles. Because of the Doppler effect, the waves reflected by moving cars have a different wavelength than those sent out by the radar device. The difference shows how fast a car is moving. Similar technology helps meteorologists [[10]] water in the atmosphere, allowing them to forecast the weather.",
